Token ID vs embedding vs contextual representation

A. Token ID
The integer assigned to a token by a tokenizer.
Example: loan → 291 (illustrative only).

B. Token embedding
A learned vector associated with that token ID, used as the starting numerical representation in a typical Transformer.
Example: loan → [0.2, -0.4, 0.7, ...].

C. Contextual representation
The representation of a token after it has been processed by Transformer layers. Its representation can reflect the surrounding words and their relationships.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- "I deposited money at the bank."
- "We sat on the river bank."
The word "bank" has the same spelling, but its meaning differs by context. Transformer layers can build representations that reflect this difference.

Important: a token embedding and a sentence embedding are not the same thing. A sentence embedding model produces a vector for a piece of text, often designed to support semantic comparison.
A language model's token embedding layer produces vectors for individual tokens.

The goal is for texts with related meanings to have vectors that are useful for measuring semantic similarity.
