Q1. What is an embedding?
An embedding is a numerical vector representation of text or other data, generated by a trained model. Embeddings are useful for semantic search, similarity comparison, clustering, and retrieval.
Q2. Why do we need embeddings in RAG?
Embeddings allow the application to represent queries and document chunks as vectors and retrieve semantically relevant information using a suitable similarity measure.
Q3. What is the difference between a token embedding and a sentence embedding?
A token embedding represents an individual token at the input embedding stage of a model. A sentence embedding represents a larger text unit, such as a sentence or paragraph, as a fixed-size vector.
Q4. What is cosine similarity?
Cosine similarity compares the angle between two vectors. It is commonly used to compare embedding vectors, although the appropriate similarity measure depends on the model and retrieval setup.
Q5. Do embeddings store the original text?
No. An embedding is a numerical representation, not a copy of the original text. RAG applications generally store the original document text or a reference to it alongside the vector so that the retrieved content can be passed to the LLM.
Q6. Can embeddings alone generate an answer?
Not in a typical retrieval system. Embeddings help find relevant information. An LLM or another downstream component can then use the retrieved information to generate or formulate an answer.

